Group A: Short Questions (2 Marks)
1. Define the term ”Sitemap” in the planning phase.
A sitemap acts as an outline that indicates the structure of the website, with all the sub-pages, main pages, and their connections. This allows the creation 
of a blueprint during the conceptual stage of making sure that the structure of the website follows logic.

2. What is the purpose of the ”Launch” phase in web development?
Launch Phase – This is the step whereby the website will be migrated from the local/staging environment to the production environment. This will involve
deploying the completely verified website copy on the production server to make it accessible by everyone. This will be done by checking the website in terms
of functionality, speed, and compatibility, after which the files will be migrated.

3. Why is ”Documentation” important in the development lifecycle?
Documentation is important, as it helps provide clarity in communications among developers, stakeholders, and developers in the future, in addition to
aiding in program debugging and making fewer bug errors.

4. Differentiate between Front-end and Back-end Development.
While front end development involves working on the user-facing components of an app or software program with technology such as HTML, CSS, or JavaScript.
Back-end development involves the server side logic, databases, and functionality. This is done through the use of languages such as PHP, Python, and Node.js.

Group B: Long Questions (4 Marks)
5. “Planning is the most critical phase of web development.”  Discuss this statement by listingthe key activities performed during the planning phase.
Planning is the most critical phase because it lays the foundation for the entire project, preventing costly changes later by aligning the website with 
business goals and user needs. It converts collected information into a structured plan.It defines websites structure, technology, and timeline.
Key activities in the planning phase are:

1. Sitemap Creation
-Sitemap: Pages - Home,
-Visual display of website organization.
2. Wire
Sketches of page layouts.
-Represents positioning of images, buttons
3. Selecting Technologies
Backend
-Backend
-Database
4. Content Strategy
-Plot all types of contents on plan.
5. SEO Strategy
-Keywords, meta tags, URL structure, mobile-friend
6. Resource Allocation
-Assign tasks to designers, devs, content writers.
7. Timeline & Milestones
-Deadlines for each stage.

6.Explain the iterative nature of the Maintenance phase. Why is a website never truly
”finished”?
Maintenance is an iterative phase since, without it, a website would be a source of security risk issues, in addition to dropping search 
engine rankings.Maintenance is ongoing from the time a site goes live. An online business requires continuous updating, monitoring, or improvements, 
rather than being a finished task.
Types of Maintenance
1. Content Updates:Blogs, price changes, or updates in the contact details.

2. Security Patches: Upgrading CMS software (such as WordPress) to avoid hacking attacks.

3. Backups:Automated backups for the database and files in the event of server failure.

4. Link Fixing:Scanning for problematic links to external pages.
A website can never be said to be "completed" or "finished" as it is a living document. This is owing to several factors that keep changing
with time such as new technology,browser updates,matters of security,new behaviors of users,business expansion,out-of-date information (such as outdated timetables 
or product information),and changes in the SEO algorithms.

